TASK: fix the bug in `d.tz` in /dayjs/src/plugin/timezone/index.js.

Current code (lines 60-66):
```js
  d.tz = function (input, timezone) {
    const previousOffset = tzOffset(+d(), timezone)
    const localTs = d.utc(input).valueOf()
    const [targetTs, targetOffset] = fixOffset(localTs, previousOffset, timezone)
    const ins = d(targetTs).utcOffset(targetOffset)
    return ins
  }
```
The bug: when `input` is NOT a string (a timestamp number, a JS Date, or a Dayjs object), the code still runs it through `fixOffset` as if it were a wall-clock time string, which double-applies the target offset. The fix (matching upstream dayjs) is to short-circuit non-string inputs so they are converted through the instance `.tz()` method:

```js
  d.tz = function (input, timezone) {
    const previousOffset = tzOffset(+d(), timezone)
    if (typeof input !== 'string') {
      // timestamp, date, dayjs
      return d(input).tz(timezone)
    }
    const localTs = d.utc(input).valueOf()
    const [targetTs, targetOffset] = fixOffset(localTs, previousOffset, timezone)
    const ins = d(targetTs).utcOffset(targetOffset)
    return ins
  }
```

